  {
    "id": 251,
    "created_at": "2023-09-15 14:57:06.016422+00",
    "title": "Things learned during dev",
    "description": "",
    "imageUrl": "http://localhost:3003/public/images/notes/red-background.jpg",
    "long_desc": {
      "time": 1703790654188,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "Y3w2hwZ-xG",
          "data": {
            "items": [
              "The nullish coalescing (??) operator is a logical operator that returns its right-hand side operand when its left-hand side operand is null or undefined, and otherwise returns its left-hand side operand."
            ],
            "style": "unordered"
          },
          "type": "list"
        },
        {
          "id": "sIEWgzLZOO",
          "data": {
            "items": [
              "CSS Grid is for layout; Flexbox is for alignment",
              "Avoid declaring arrow functions or binding in render for optimal performance.<br>",
              "By default, Tailwind uses a mobile-first breakpoint system. What this means is that unprefixed utilities (like uppercase) take effect on all screen sizes, while prefixed utilities (like md:uppercase) only take effect at the specified breakpoint and above."
            ],
            "style": "unordered"
          },
          "type": "list"
        },
        {
          "id": "eRGb1hyIul",
          "data": {
            "items": [
              "The target property returns the element on which the event occurred, opposed to the currentTarget property, which returns the element whose event listener triggered the event."
            ],
            "style": "unordered"
          },
          "type": "list"
        },
        {
          "id": "Ki2hPDZgVj",
          "data": {
            "text": "Strict equality (===) operator",
            "level": 1
          },
          "type": "header"
        },
        {
          "id": "9E-EXBe7Dr",
          "data": {
            "text": "checks whether its two operands are equal, returning a Boolean result. It always considers operands of different types to be different."
          },
          "type": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "id": "w-lD8NMYBi",
          "data": {
            "text": "&lt;img loading=\"lazy\"/&gt;",
            "level": 1
          },
          "type": "header"
        },
        {
          "id": "_K3R1rpm9v",
          "data": {
            "text": "Load the images as you scroll,check the network tab"
          },
          "type": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "id": "4L58_-SUq1",
          "data": {
            "text": "Rendering patterns",
            "level": 1
          },
          "type": "header"
        },
        {
          "id": "g3xxqulNyI",
          "data": {
            "text": "A rendering pattern refers to the way in which the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code is all processed and rendered in a website. Different rendering patterns are used to achieve different performance and user experience goals. The most common rendering patterns in web development are: Client-side rendering (CSR): In CSR, the client's browser generates the HTML content of a web page on the client-side using JavaScript. This approach can provide a fast and interactive user experience but can be slower for initial loading times and bad for SEO. Server-side rendering (SSR): In SSR, the web server generates the HTML content of a web page on the server-side and sends it to the client's browser. This approach can improve initial loading times and SEO (search engine optimization) but can be slower for dynamic content. Server-Side Rendering (SSR) can help improve the initial load time of the application by rendering the initial page on the server-side. This can help improve the Time-to-Interactive (TTI) and First Contentful Paint (FCP) metrics. You can use tools such as Next.js or Gatsby to implement SSR in your application. If your priority is to provide users with a fluid, native-like experience, CSR is probably a good choice. If you need ultra fast page load times, SSR or SSG can be a better option."
          },
          "type": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "id": "_hkMFTXnxu",
          "data": {
            "text": "npm outdated",
            "level": 1
          },
          "type": "header"
        },
        {
          "id": "gp7_f6f-qp",
          "data": {
            "text": "This command will check every installed dependency and compare the current version with the latest version in the npm registry."
          },
          "type": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "id": "LhX8GkSTCl",
          "data": {
            "text": "npm update",
            "level": 1
          },
          "type": "header"
        },
        {
          "id": "AJlBCfQ47H",
          "data": {
            "text": "With this method, to install updates for every package, you just need to run: npm update Keep in mind that with npm update it will never update to a major breaking-changes version. It updates the dependencies in package.json and package-lock.json. It will use the \"wanted\" version. To obtain the \"latest\" version append @latest to individual installs, for example npm install react@latest."
          },
          "type": "paragraph"
        }
      ],
      "version": "2.28.2"
    },
    "user_id": "05244198-7873-488e-a8c5-e9e7646d7df2"
  },
